        <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
          <span>Why is 1 micro equal to 1,000 nano?</span>
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer" style="display: none;">
          <p>In the International System of Units (SI), metric prefixes follow powers of ten in steps of 10³. Micro is 10⁻⁶ and nano is 10⁻⁹. The mathematical ratio between 10⁻⁶ and 10⁻⁹ is 10³ = 1,000.</p>
          <div class="faq-formula">
            <code>10⁻⁶ ÷ 10⁻⁹ = 10³ = 1,000</code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ Item 6 -->
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
          <span>Where are nano and micro units used in real life?</span>
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer" style="display: none;">
          <p>Nanometers (nm) measure semiconductor transistors, DNA helix widths (2.5 nm), and light wavelengths. Micrometers (µm) measure biological cells (10–30 µm), human hair diameter (70 µm), and optical fibers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ Item 7 -->
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
          <span>How does nano-to-micro metrology apply to antigravity research?</span>
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer" style="display: none;">
          <p>Precision testing of hypothetical gravitational anomalies, Casimir cavity forces, and propellantless thrust requires micro-Newton (µN) and nano-Newton (nN) laser interferometry and torsion balances to rule out thermal expansion and air currents.</p>
          <div class="faq-formula">
            <code>1 µN = 1,000 nN = 10⁻⁶ N</code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    document.querySelectorAll('.faq-accordion .faq-question').forEach((button) => {
      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const item = button.closest('.faq-item');
        const answer = item?.querySelector('.faq-answer');
        const isExpanded = button.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';

        if (isExpanded) {
          button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
          if (answer instanceof HTMLElement) answer.style.display = 'none';
          item?.classList.remove('open');
        } else {
          button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'true');
          if (answer instanceof HTMLElement) answer.style.display = 'block';
          item?.classList.add('open');
        }
      });
    });

    // Scroll animation observer
    if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
      const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
        entries.forEach((entry) => {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            entry.target.classList.add('visible');
            observer.unobserve(entry.target);
          }
        });
      }, { threshold: 0.1 });

      document.querySelectorAll('.animate-on-scroll').forEach((el) => observer.observe(el));
    }
  </script>

